Hex Workshop Layout
Figure 1.1 Hex Workshop Layout
1. Tool Bar: The toolbar is the row of icons at the top of Hex Workshop.
It provides quick access to commonly used functions such as:
• Open / Save file
• Undo / Redo
• Cut / Copy / Paste
• Search / Replace
• Bookmarks
• Checksum tools
• Structure Viewer
• Compare files
2. Data Visualizer: The Data Visualizer helps you view selected data in various representations,
such as:
• Bitmap view (visual pattern of bytes)
• Decimal / Hex graphs
• Raw binary visualization
This is useful to identify patterns, signatures, or embedded data.
3. Editor Window: This is the main hex editing area. It contains:
• Offset column (addresses)
• Hexadecimal data column
• ASCII/text column
4. Data Inspector: The Data Inspector automatically interprets the selected bytes and shows
them as:
• Signed / Unsigned integers
• Floating-point numbers
• Binary
• ASCII / Unicode
• Date/time values
Very useful for understanding raw binary data.
5. Expression Calc: A built-in calculator used for:
• Evaluating arithmetic & logical expressions
• Converting between bases (hex, decimal, binary)
• Working with offsets and ranges
• Performing checksum calculations
Ideal for reverse engineering and data analysis.
6. Structure View: This pane lets you apply C-style structures (.structure files) to the binary.
Used for parsing:
• File headers
• Metadata blocks
• Network packets
• Custom binary formats
7. Results Window: Displays the output from different tools:
• Search results
• Compare results
• Bookmarks list
• Script outputs
• Checksum reports
Acts as a log or analysis summary.
8. Status Bar: Located at the bottom. It displays:
• Current cursor position (offset)
• Selected byte range
• Edit mode (overwrite/insert)
• File size
• Hex/ASCII status
Helps keep track of editing activity.
